The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
end_requests(fc, &fpq->processing);
fuse_abort_conn(fc, false);
```
Reason:
The function `fuse_dev_release()` calls `end_requests()` to process the `fpq->processing` list without any locking mechanism, assuming it is the only function accessing it. However, `fuse_abort_conn()` can also access this list concurrently, leading to potential race conditions and undefined behavior when both functions attempt to modify or read from the list simultaneously.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
spin_lock(&fpq->lock);
list_splice_init(&fpq->processing, &to_end);
spin_unlock(&fpq->lock);
```
Reason:
The patch introduces a spin lock around the access to the `fpq->processing` list, ensuring that only one function can modify or read from the list at a time. This prevents race conditions by serializing access to the list, thus ensuring safe operations when both `fuse_dev_release()` and `fuse_abort_conn()` are called.

